  "prediction": "**Apple's Decision to Remove Headphone Jack Sparks Outrage**\n\nAt the recent Apple keynote event, the company announced the removal of the standard 3.5mm aux cable input, also known as the headphone jack, from its latest iPhone 7 model. Marketing chief Phil Schiller explained the decision by stating that it was a matter of \"courage\" to move on and do something better for all users. However, his words were met with widespread mockery and criticism on social media.\n\nCritics argue that Apple's decision is not about innovation or progress, but rather about profit and control. The removal of the headphone jack forces users to purchase expensive and unwieldy dongles or wireless headphones, such as Apple's AirPods, which cost $159. This decision has been seen as a move to further Apple's walled garden approach, where users are forced to use proprietary products and services.\n\nMany experts and users have pointed out that there is no technological excuse for removing the headphone jack, as music does not sound better over a Lightning cable or wireless technology. In fact, wired audio is generally considered to be of higher quality than wireless audio.\n\nThe decision has also been criticized for its impact on users who rely on the headphone jack for other devices, such as Square's credit card dongle, temperature sensors, light meters, thermal imaging cameras, and personal breathalyzers. These users will now be forced to use a proprietary Lightning connector, which may not be compatible with their devices.\n\nThe removal of the headphone jack has been seen as a step backward for Apple, which has traditionally been known for its user-friendly and seamless experience. The company's mantra of \"it just works\" is now officially dead, as users will have to navigate a complex and proprietary ecosystem to use their headphones.\n\nOverall, Apple's decision to remove the headphone jack has sparked widespread outrage and criticism, with many users and experts calling it a move to further Apple's profit margins and control over its users.",
